{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-65571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-65571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(\\frac{3}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-65571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f6fc0a52c49ef15f3fb933b08e6bc14520d02f2f2dac8bbfc900f683b3b371f"} {"question" "q-65571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-65571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-65571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-79194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/a41a721eab2eb97e8b7b30ddf877e37a48d2b74eeef82ab0a4b72f3bb4a93603.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-79194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f08846ce1f79cd14c593fbf37360fb8169a26704c956506ca0deedb8025e3b39.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-79194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f6fc0a52c49ef15f3fb933b08e6bc14520d02f2f2dac8bbfc900f683b3b371f"} {"question" "q-79194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-79194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-79194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-142000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/98edb404419e7ab515109f87addb44709513007b1a0105b648a3ce2b62ad547f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-142000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+1=4.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-142000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f6fc0a52c49ef15f3fb933b08e6bc14520d02f2f2dac8bbfc900f683b3b371f"} {"question" "q-142000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-142000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-142000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f6ee30d5c189c9579a1f38d59f06581a7cfff944d42489eab2e3c0c6ce089ad4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+(-\\frac{12}{5})=-\\frac{9}{5}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f6fc0a52c49ef15f3fb933b08e6bc14520d02f2f2dac8bbfc900f683b3b371f"} {"question" "q-143668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{6}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{9}{5}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{9}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/ee4ad78545160a052a28f0f0fcc7df476cc2063e48429a315a9cb9242f7d5311.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/e1ed3a322c82b5c70acb01c54c97b09814f41aec80d980034a6fcdc46037209c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f6fc0a52c49ef15f3fb933b08e6bc14520d02f2f2dac8bbfc900f683b3b371f"} {"question" "q-143674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"} {"question" "q-143674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f699d4be1d8223cc42d7ea3b656e016d00248b49f7f0502da4c3c4b392672fa"}] "usage" {"q-79194" []} "basis" 2433 "retractions" [[17592186184788 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3